DESCRIPTION:Instructions\n\nChoose one of the problem briefs below and resp
 ond to it individually. This is your opportunity to demonstrate how well y
 ou can apply this module's tools and thinking models to a real-world compl
 ex challenge. Your task is to carry out a full problem solving analysis in
  a slide document that includes the following parts:\n\nPart A: Identify &
  Frame\n\n*\n\n*\n\n*\n\n*\n\n* Highlight the complex problem within your 
 selected problem brief (see the last section of this assignment and choose
  your problem brief) & identify the GCGO it falls under.\n\n* Analyze what
  traits makes it complex enough to impact the lives of millions of Rwandan
 s.\n\n* Draft out a 250 words "Problem Overview" document that explains th
 e selected problem thoroughly. (save as PDF in Google Drive then link)\n\n
 * Identify at least 3 different stakeholders (e.g. 2 primary stakeholder
 s & 1 Secondary stakeholder)\n\n*\n\n* List out 5 key stakeholder intervie
 w questions for Primary stakeholders\n\n* List out 5 key stakeholder inter
 view questions for Secondary stakeholder (s)\n\n* Engage with all listed s
 takeholders and combine recordings of the interviews in a collage (as audi
 o and video with subtitles. Save MP4 in Google Drive & then link in slide)
 \n\nPart B: Reframe & Probe\n\n*\n\n*\n\n*\n\n*\n\n* Analyze Your Findings
  using an Empathy Map to understand what your stakeholders Say\, Do\, Thin
 k\, and Feel.\n\n* Showcase Pains & Gains insights clearly from the empath
 y map.\n\n* Synthesize your insights into a clear\, user-centered POV stat
 ement in the correct format.\n\n* Draft 2-3 optimistic "How Might We" (HMW
 ) open-ended questions that reframe potential challenges based on your POV
 .\n\n* Apply the 5 Whys Root Cause Analysis technique to your POV to dig p
 ast surface-level symptoms to the root cause.\n\nPart C: Generate Interven
 tions\n\n*\n\n*\n\n*\n\n*\n\n* Draft an appropriate Problem Statement (3
 –4 sentences\, data/fact-informed & use the 5Ws)\n\n* Highlight one key 
 tension (polarities) present in the system and explain why you made this s
 election.\n\n* Analyze the identified tension using a Polarity Map.\n\n* U
 se the balancing insights from the map to generate 5 best interventions th
 at solve the problem\n\n* Identify the top 2 interventions with a detailed
  Decision Matrix analysis. \n\nPart D: Plan Your Experiment\n\n*\n\n*\n\n
 *\n\n*\n\n* Describe the Solution Statements for the top 2 interventions i
 n the matrix analysis.\n\n* Plan out a safe-to-fail low-risk experiments t
 hat would be tested over 4 weeks for the 1st intervention. (Ensure to expl
 ain the rationale\, assumptions\, risks\, success signals that include the
  performance indicators or key metrics etc.).\n\n* Plan out a safe-to-fail
  low-risk experiments that would be tested over 4 weeks for the 2nd interv
 ention. (Ensure to explain the rationale\, assumptions\, risks\, success s
 ignals that include the performance indicators or key metrics etc.).\n\nPa
 rt E: Your Personal Reflection\n\n*\n\n*\n\n*\n\n*\n\n* Reflect briefly on
  how this assignment challenged your thinking of the problem you selected.
 \n\n* Identify which frameworks\, tool or mindset change that helped you t
 he most and explains how.\n\nPart F: Citations of sources\, Use of AI\, an
 d Academic Integrity\n\n*\n\n*\n\n*\n\n*\n\n* Include your in-text citatio
 ns and reference list of external and self-made analysis sources in APA7 s
 tyle strictly.\n\n* Be sure to include at least five reliable sources (t
 he sources have to be among the following: peer-reviewed articles\, offici
 al documents from governments or relevant organizations). \n\n* You can u
 se AI tools to brainstorm or organize your work (e.g. design your slides)\
 , but do not copy-paste AI-generated answers as your own. If you happen t
 o use AI\, include an annex section after the reference list showing all
  the prompts and outputs you used in your brainstorming.\n\n* Using Genera
 tive AI tools and not recognizing it will be considered as academic miscon
 duct.\n\n \n\nSubmission Requirements:\n\n* Format: Slides in PDF\n\n* Ma
 ximum of 20 slides (Slide 1 should include Title of Problem and your name.
  Slide 20 for the References in APA 7)\n\n* Fonts: 1.5 spacing\, 12pt+ fon
 t-size.\n\n* Max of 200 words per slide.\n\n* Originality: Your framing\, 
 analysis\, and intervention design must be your own.\n\n* Citation: Refere
 nce should be cited in APA7 style.\n\n \n\n \n\nChoose One of the Follow
 ing Problem Briefs\n\n* Youth in Kigali’s Waste Recycling Sector Startup
  Success Limitations\n\n* Circular economy initiatives are rising\, and ma
 ny young people are eager to join the recycling sector. However\, the indu
 stry remains informal\, underfinanced\, and stigmatized. Youth-led startup
 s struggle with licensing\, waste collection contracts\, and finding relia
 ble markets for recycled materials.\n\n* Key challenges: Informality\, reg
 ulatory barriers\, market instability\, low public awareness about sorting
  waste.\n\n \n\n* Affordable Housing Gaps in Fast-Growing Towns within Rw
 anda\n\n* As secondary towns like Rubavu and Nyagatare grow\, demand for a
 ffordable housing rises. Yet low-income families struggle to access mortga
 ge products\, and construction costs remain high. Informal settlements exp
 and as formal developers focus on middle- and upper-income markets.\n\n* K
 ey challenges: Housing affordability\, limited financing options\, rising 
 urbanization\, market mismatch.\n\n \n\n* Technical and Vocational Educat
 ion Students Leaving Before Graduation\n\n* Technical and Vocational Educa
 tion and Training (TVET) is central to Rwanda’s industrialization agenda
 . Yet dropout rates remain high\, especially among students from low-incom
 e backgrounds. Many cite financial constraints\, long commutes\, family pr
 essure\, or lack of clarity about job prospects. Institutions attempt to p
 rovide career guidance\, but the support is inconsistent.\n\n* Key challen
 ges: Education financing gaps\, weak support systems\, unclear pathways to
  employment\, socio-economic pressures.
